## 3b. Schema versioning (prerequisite for section 4, applies once section 0 marks the database as Active)

**Platform-neutral by design.** This kit does not assume a database vendor. Fill `{{MIGRATIONS_DIR}}` with wherever this project's migrations actually live — `supabase/migrations/`, `prisma/migrations/`, `db/migrate/`, or anything else — and name the migration tool this project uses. What the kit requires is not a specific platform, but that schema changes are **versioned files** and that a **disposable sandbox** exists.
- [ ] The migrations folder (`{{MIGRATIONS_DIR}}`) exists, along with whatever config file this project's migration tool needs
- [ ] Migrations reflect the actual current production schema — **(retrofit only)** if schema was ever built ad hoc (changes applied directly through a dashboard or SQL console and never captured in git), baseline it now by pulling the live schema into a first migration with this project's own tooling. Check for the gap by comparing what the platform reports as applied against what's in `{{MIGRATIONS_DIR}}` locally. The individual history of prior ad hoc changes is not recoverable — the pulled baseline becomes the new starting point, and that's an acceptable one-time cost.
- [ ] Confirmed going forward every schema change is a migration file created with this project's migration tool, never SQL run directly against any database by hand
- [ ] `check-migrations-schema-only.mjs` (lives in `.claude/skills/verify-rls/scripts/`) installed as a pre-commit hook (via Husky or the `pre-commit` framework — same mechanism as the `gitleaks` hook in section 4b) so a migration mixing schema and real-data changes is blocked before it's committed, not caught later
- [ ] `migrations-schema-only-workflow.yml.template` copied to `.github/workflows/migrations-schema-only.yml` — the CI backstop for anything that slips past the hook (unhooked clone, `--no-verify`, a direct push)
- [ ] The production migrations log created from `PROD_MIGRATIONS_LOG.md.template`, alongside the migrations folder — every migration applied to production gets a row logged at the moment it happens, not after (see `verify-rls` skill section 2)
- [ ] **Do not check any box in section 4 (Sandbox) until this section is fully checked** — a reproducible sandbox requires reproducible migrations; without this, "sandbox" setup degrades into manually eyeballing two databases, which is the fragile process this framework exists to avoid

