## 4b. Secrets hygiene
- [ ] `.env.example` copied in, real values filled into `.env.local` (the actual convention this stack's tooling auto-loads and treats as git-ignorable — never invent a name like `.env.dev`), never into `.env.example` itself, and never into a bare `.env` either
- [ ] `.env*` (except `.env.example`) confirmed present in `.gitignore` — explicitly check both `.env` and `.env.local` are listed, not just one
- [ ] Confirmed no API key, password, or connection string exists in `CLAUDE.md`, `SETUP_CHECKLIST.md`, `TASKS.md`, or any file under `.claude/skills/`
- [ ] **(retrofit only, mandatory before anything else in this section counts as done)** Ran a real content-scanning tool across the full git history — `gitleaks git .` (not just a filename grep like `git log -- .env` — a real leak was found once already sitting in a plain `docs/*.md` file, which a filename-based check would never catch, since secrets can hide in *any* tracked file: docs, READMEs, comments, seed scripts, not just conventional env/key files). **If `gitleaks` itself can't be installed in the current execution environment** (e.g. a cloud agent sandbox with restricted network access to GitHub/the Go proxy — confirmed to happen), an equivalent full-content scanner across the same full history (`detect-secrets` plus a manual regex sweep of the complete diff, for example) satisfies this requirement equally — the bar is a real content scan across every commit, not the specific binary. **If anything turns up: the credential must be rotated or disabled at its source immediately.** Untracking the file or editing it out afterward is cleanup, not remediation — it does not undo the exposure. A warning comment written next to a secret ("don't commit this") is not a safeguard and does not count as mitigation. Do not check this box until rotation/disabling is confirmed done, not just scheduled.
- [ ] `gitleaks` (or equivalent) installed as a pre-commit hook (via Husky or the `pre-commit` framework — `gitleaks protect --staged` on every commit) so a secret is blocked *before* it lands on a branch, not caught later in a periodic scan. This is the first structural fix; the scan above is the one-time catch-up for history that already exists.
- [ ] `gitleaks-workflow.yml.template` copied to `.github/workflows/gitleaks.yml` — the CI backstop that catches anything the local hook misses (unhooked clone, `--no-verify`, a direct push), visible as a pass/fail check on every PR. Runs on GitHub's own runners, so it's unaffected by any network restriction in whatever environment ran the one-time history scan above. Not a replacement for the pre-commit hook above; both matter, since either one alone can be bypassed.

## 4c. Trigger points — the rules that fire on their own

**Why this section exists, and why it's a section rather than a note.** The most expensive failure this framework has recorded is not a rule being wrong — it's a correct rule that nothing ever executed. A verification step required by `CLAUDE.md` went **unrun across five consecutive commits** because the skill that should have called it ended one step earlier; a session-closing rule went unexecuted for weeks because the skill implementing it had six steps and none of them was the rule. Both were written down. Both were correct. Neither fired. **A rule with no trigger does not exist**, so every rule that *can* be mechanical gets a line here.

- [ ] **Pre-commit chain installed**, running every applicable check in one hook: secret scanning (section 4b), migration schema-only enforcement (section 3b), and any project-specific integrity check (tracker index vs. its source files, generated artifacts vs. what they're generated from). Prefer a **versioned hooks directory** (`git config core.hooksPath`) over a package-manager-specific tool when the project isn't already on that package manager — the hooks then travel with the repo instead of depending on an install step. Note the one cost: a fresh clone must run the `core.hooksPath` command once.
- [ ] **Pre-push runs the project's own gate** (lint, tests, type check — whatever CI would run). **This matters most for a project that pushes straight to its deployed branch:** CI and the host's build both run *after* the push, so by the time they complain the tracked branch is already red and the deploy already blocked. The hook moves that barrier to before anything leaves the machine.
- [ ] **CI backstops exist for every local hook.** A local hook can be bypassed with `--no-verify` and doesn't exist at all in a fresh clone. Duplicating the check in CI is deliberate, not redundant: the hook tells you fast, CI is the one that can't be skipped.
- [ ] **Deploy/CI status verification is wired into a step something actually reaches** — not left as an instruction to remember after pushing. See the `ship` skill's terminal condition.
- [ ] **Every non-negotiable rule in `CLAUDE.md` names the skill or hook that executes it**, and each one has been traced once to confirm that step is really reached. A rule whose trigger can't be named is either not yet implemented or not really a rule — mark which.

**One line per checker, and this is the part that was missing.** The five boxes above are about hooks and CI. They never mention a checker by name, so a project could tick all five with six kit scripts sitting on disk that nothing calls — which is exactly what was measured in a real project: seven checkers installed, three wired, and the kit reporting green over the other four. Delivering a checker nobody runs is worse than not delivering it: it installs the belief that the rule is covered.

Tick each one only when its trigger is real. If a checker doesn't apply to this project, **declare it in `.ae-kit-excluded` with its reason AND do not write its rule row** — both halves, because they answer different questions. The exclusion list stops the installer from delivering the file; not writing the row is what stops `CLAUDE.md` from claiming a rule that nothing fires. `check-rule-triggers.mjs` **never reads `.ae-kit-excluded`**: a row stays red for as long as the row exists. What is not legitimate is leaving the file on disk with nothing calling it.

- [ ] `scripts/generate-task-index.mjs` — **pre-commit**, and duplicated in CI · line to add: `node scripts/generate-task-index.mjs --check`
- [ ] `scripts/check-task-links.mjs` — **pre-commit**, and duplicated in CI · line to add: `node scripts/check-task-links.mjs`
- [ ] `scripts/check-status-shape.mjs` — **pre-commit** · line to add: `node scripts/check-status-shape.mjs`. Exit `3` (old layout, not migrated yet) should warn without blocking
- [ ] `scripts/check-log-layout.mjs` — **pre-commit** · line to add: `node scripts/check-log-layout.mjs`. Exit `3` (session logs still in the flat pre-0.34.0 layout) and exit `2` (no `logs/` yet) should both warn without blocking — a project migrates its logs when it decides to, not because it updated the kit. It also checks that every reference to a session log resolves, which nothing else in the kit does. A document that quotes pre-migration paths on purpose — a migration inventory, a procedure — declares `<!-- check-log-layout: ignore -->` on a line of its own and is skipped whole; the checker counts the exemptions in its summary line on every run, so an exemption stays visible rather than silent, and `--verbose` lists each skipped file. Two exceptions it makes on its own: a dead reference inside a **committed** task log entry is reported without blocking, because committed entries are never edited (a line not yet committed still blocks); and the **content** of `logs/subagents/` is never read, though that folder may only hold `YYYY-MM-DD/` folders, `collector-errors.log` and a `.gitignore` — anything else there blocks. Session lessons live in `logs/lessons/`, which holds only `lesson-payload-YYYY-MM-DD.json` (written by the close) and `lesson-payload.md` (written by `scripts/emit-lessons.mjs`); anything else in there blocks, because the folder is new in 0.36.0 and has no inherited files to respect. A lesson file still loose in `logs/` from before 0.36.0 is **warned about and never blocked** — the checker names each one and prints the single command that moves them, so a project migrates when it decides to
- [ ] `scripts/check-task-log.mjs` — **pre-commit** · line to add: `node scripts/check-task-log.mjs`. This is the line rule `R35` names; without it `check-rule-triggers.mjs` exits 1 with `hook "pre-commit" exists but does not run "check-task-log"`, and the cheap way out is deleting the row, which is the degradation `R28` exists to stop. Exit `2` (no task has a task log yet — the skills create the first one) and exit `3` (the module that reads the format is missing, an incomplete install) should both warn without blocking
- [ ] `scripts/generate-task-log-view.mjs` — **pre-commit** · line to add: `node scripts/generate-task-log-view.mjs --check-hand-edits`. Same shape as the task index: `logs/tasks/current/<task-id>.md` is generated, and `--check-hand-edits` is what protects it: the view is regenerated at session close, and the hook only refuses a view somebody edited by hand. Exit `2` and exit `3` are the same two "nothing to do here" cases as above and should warn without blocking. **These two checkers, and the task log they check, cannot be declared out**: the skills record every step of the work through the task log, so the installer installs them even when `.ae-kit-excluded` names them, and `R35` stays
- [ ] `scripts/check-local-adjustments.mjs` — **pre-commit** · line to add: `node scripts/check-local-adjustments.mjs || [ $? -eq 3 ]`. This is the line rule `R38` names; without it `check-rule-triggers.mjs` exits 1 on that row. It blocks (exit `1`) a commit where a kit file changed since it was installed, a row of `CLAUDE.md` → Local kit adjustments is incomplete, or a local adjustment file is not named by any row. Exit `3` (no footprint: `.ae-kit-manifest` is missing because the project was installed before this version, or the last install could not write it — no SHA-256 tool, or the file could not be written — and said so) should warn without blocking, like `check-status-shape`: kit files could not be compared, which is a state, not a defect — every install that gets through copying the kit writes the footprint. A project that already edited a kit file converts that edit into a row before wiring this line, following `ae-kit-templates/local-adjustments.md`
- [ ] `scripts/check-commit-task.mjs` — **commit-msg**, a hook of its own next to pre-commit (`.githooks/commit-msg` or `.husky/commit-msg`, whichever this project uses) · line to add: `node scripts/check-commit-task.mjs "$1"`. It refuses a commit whose message does not end with a `Task: <id>` line naming a task in `tasks/` (or `Task: none`), or whose `Unit: U<n>` line, next to `Task:`, names a unit that task does not have in the task file staged for the commit (the subject is not read); the skills that commit write both lines. Exit `2` (it could not check: no `tasks/`, no git) should warn without blocking, and so should a machine with no `node` on the hook's PATH — write the hook as `if command -v node >/dev/null; then node scripts/check-commit-task.mjs "$1" || [ $? -eq 2 ]; else echo "no node: the Task: line was not checked" >&2; fi`, which blocks only on exit `1`. A merge commit (`git merge`, or a `git pull` whose history diverged) is let through without the line, with a warning, while the merge is in progress: git writes that message itself, and holding a task back refuses an unpushed merge anyway. Without this hook nothing checks the line at commit time, and holding a task back refuses to run over a commit that lacks it: the safe side, but found late. A fresh clone runs `git config core.hooksPath` once for this hook as for pre-commit
- [ ] **A CI workflow exists that runs the three checkers below.** ⚠️ **The kit does not ship one** — it ships `gitleaks-workflow.yml.template` and (for a database project) the migrations workflow, and nothing else. Until this job exists, the three rows below have nowhere to run and must not be written into `CLAUDE.md`. The job needs a step per checker, named so the rule's trigger can find it: a `run:` line containing `check-rule-triggers`, one containing `check-skill-outputs`, and — where there is application code — one containing `check-inline-rules`. The rule rows name the workflow **filename**, so whatever this file is called, the rows must match it
- [ ] `scripts/check-rule-triggers.mjs` — **CI** · step: `run: node scripts/check-rule-triggers.mjs`. The one that checks all the others; a project where only this one runs still learns what is missing
- [ ] `scripts/check-skill-outputs.mjs` — **CI** · step: `run: node scripts/check-skill-outputs.mjs`
- [ ] `scripts/check-inline-rules.mjs` — **CI** · step: `run: node scripts/check-inline-rules.mjs` — add `--lib PATH_HERE` unless this project's pure logic really is at `src/lib`, which is the only path it looks for by default; without it the step exits 2 on every run. Only where there is application code to scan
- [ ] `scripts/check-deploy-verified.mjs` — **`open-session`**, not a hook: git has no event for "I started working". The skill already runs it; nothing to add unless this project's `open-session` was rewritten
- [ ] `scripts/check-pendings.mjs` — **`open-session`**, same reason
- [ ] `scripts/check-kit-current.mjs` — **`open-session`**, same reason
- [ ] `scripts/collect-subagent.mjs` — **a Claude Code hook**, which is neither of the two kinds above: it is declared under `SubagentStop` in `.claude/settings.json`, and Claude Code runs it when a subagent stops. **`install.sh` writes that declaration itself** — it creates `.claude/settings.json` if the project has none, and adds only the `SubagentStop` event if the file exists without one — so in the normal case there is nothing to add here and this box is ticked from the installer's report. **If the installer reported a conflict** it changed nothing and printed the exact block to paste. It reports one for any of these, not only the obvious two: the project already declares its own `SubagentStop`; the file does not parse as JSON; the file cannot be read, or its top level is not an object; `hooks`, or the event under it, has an unexpected shape; there is no `node` on the machine to run the merge; or — the one that surprises people — **the file's formatting is one the installer cannot reproduce byte for byte**, such as tabs or four-space indentation, in which case it refuses to rewrite the file even though no hook of yours is at stake. Whatever the cause, the install is **not complete** until that block is in `.claude/settings.json`, because a project without the hook collects no subagent evidence at all — reviewer reports then live only in Claude Code's own transcripts, which are deleted after `cleanupPeriodDays`. This is the line rule `R36` names, `claude-hook:SubagentStop:collect-subagent.mjs`, and the checker reads **only** `.claude/settings.json`, which therefore has to be **versioned**: a project whose `.gitignore` excludes `.claude/` will be green on the machine that installed and red in CI, with a message saying the file does not exist — the repair there is to track the file, not to write it again. Declaring the hook in `settings.local.json` leaves the row red on purpose, since that file is not versioned and would be green on one machine and red in CI. Declared out in `.ae-kit-excluded` → the installer writes no hook, and the `R36` row is not written either — both halves, as above. **When secret scanning blocks a commit because a saved transcript quoted a credential, the procedure is in the header comment of `scripts/collect-subagent.mjs`** (redact in place, rotate at the provider, re-stage, never `--no-verify`); it lives next to the code that writes those files rather than being copied here, where the two versions would drift apart
- [ ] `.claude/skills/verify-rls/scripts/check-migrations-schema-only.mjs` — **pre-commit** · line to add: `node .claude/skills/verify-rls/scripts/check-migrations-schema-only.mjs --migrations-dir PATH_HERE`. **Pass that flag unless this project's migrations really live in one of the six folders it autodetects** (`supabase/migrations`, `prisma/migrations`, `drizzle`, `db/migrate`, `migrations`, `sql/migrations`): with none of them present it finds no files, prints nothing and exits 0, so the hook installs, the rule row goes green and no migration is ever checked. Unlike the `--lib` case above, this one fails **silently**. Only if this project has a database. Note the path: this one ships inside the skill that owns it, not in `scripts/`
> **Where those lines go, and how to add them so they can actually block.** The pre-commit ones are added to this project's existing hook — `.husky/pre-commit` or `.githooks/pre-commit`, whichever this project uses — appended to what is already there, never replacing it. **The hook must start with `set -eu`** (a Husky `pre-commit` is a plain `sh` script that does not, by default — and write `set -eu`, not `set -euo pipefail`: `pipefail` is a bash option, and under `dash`, which is `/bin/sh` on Debian, Ubuntu and most CI runners, the hook dies on its first line with `Illegal option -o pipefail` before a single checker runs. Use the longer form only in a hook whose shebang is bash): without it a hook's exit status is the status of its **last** line, so every checker above the last one prints its complaint and the commit goes through anyway. Measured, not theorised: with these lines appended to a bare hook, a checker exiting 2 left the hook exiting 0. If you cannot add `set -e` to that hook, chain the lines with `&&` instead. Note that `check-rule-triggers.mjs` cannot see this: it looks for the command's text inside the hook file, so all these rows go green whether or not the line can ever block. The CI ones are steps in the workflow job from the box above. The rule row must name the hook file and the workflow **filename** this project actually has, because that is the string `check-rule-triggers.mjs` looks for.

> **What a `ci:` trigger proves, and what it does not.** `ci:ci.yml:check-skill-outputs` checks two things: that `.github/workflows/ci.yml` exists, and that the text `check-skill-outputs` appears **somewhere in that file**. Anywhere. A mention inside a `#` comment counts. A step inside a job guarded by `if: false`, or one that only ever runs on `workflow_dispatch`, counts. So a workflow that names the three checkers in a TODO comment and runs none of them comes out green on all three rows — including the row that certifies this checker itself runs. Measured, not theorised. And the two-part form — `ci:ci.yml`, with no command after the filename — checks **only that the file exists**, with no command to look for at all; the same goes for `hook:pre-commit` written without its command. That matters here because the row this kit ships for secret scanning is exactly that shape (`ci:{{SECRET_SCAN_WORKFLOW}}`, one placeholder, nowhere to put a command), so it stays green over a workflow file whose entire contents are a comment. Write the three-part form wherever you can. Tick the box above from the workflow you actually read, not from the exit code: at best the exit code confirms the row points at a real file whose text contains the command, and stops there. Hardening this is open work, not a decision already taken.

- [ ] **Run `node scripts/check-rule-triggers.mjs` and get exit 0.** Every `script:` trigger names the skill that runs it, and the checker reads that skill's `SKILL.md` to confirm the script is **named** there. Be exact about the verb: it proves the row names a real script and a real caller that mentions it — which catches a row whose script was never installed or was excluded, a row pointing at a directory, and the pre-0.30.0 form that named no caller at all. It does **not** prove the caller runs it. A `SKILL.md` that mentions a script only to say it stopped using it still passes, and a kit install delivers the script and the `SKILL.md` naming it as one unit, so these rows are green from the moment they are installed. Whether the caller actually runs it is what the `verification` column's artifact answers — the exit code in a log — not this checker.

⚠️ **Be exact about what that exit 0 proves, because the gap it leaves is the one this section exists to close.** `check-rule-triggers.mjs` only ever looks at rules that are IN the table. **A checker sitting in `scripts/` with no rule naming it produces no row, no finding, and exit 0** — it is invisible, which is precisely the measured failure that motivated this section (seven checkers installed, three wired, everything reporting green). So the exit 0 confirms the rows you wrote; **the boxes above are what confirm you wrote a row for every checker on disk**, and nothing mechanical checks that yet. Walk the list against `ls scripts/` once, by hand, and tick these boxes from what you see rather than from what the checker said.

