## Sandbox — reference block

**The requirement is a genuinely separate, disposable environment — not a flag, not a boolean in config.** How you get one depends on the platform: some offer ephemeral per-branch databases (often paid-tier only), and the fallback that works everywhere is a **second, separate instance** seeded with fake data. The trade is per-feature isolation for one persistent shared dev instance; the goal is intact either way, because production credentials simply never exist in the dev environment rather than being toggled off.

Use this block in section 4, filling in the platform's own terms:

```
- [ ] Sandbox: separate dev/sandbox instance created (name: ___, connection details in .env.local)
- [ ] Sandbox: seeded with fake data
- [ ] Sandbox: reset script written and tested (path: ___)
- [ ] Sandbox: production credentials confirmed absent from the dev environment entirely
- [ ] App code reads its connection from the environment with NO hardcoded fallback — a missing variable fails loudly at startup instead of silently connecting to something unintended
```

> That last item comes from a real gap: a project ran for weeks with production credentials hardcoded as a fallback in its client setup, so every local dev run touched live user data. A sandbox that the app can silently bypass is not a sandbox.
